<BaseLayout title="About Acupressure Guide" description="Learn about Acupressure Guide, our mission to make evidence-based acupressure information accessible, and how we approach accuracy and transparency." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "About", href: "/about" }]}>
<article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">About Acupressure Guide</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Our mission is to make evidence-based acupressure information accessible to everyone. We bridge the gap between the traditional understanding of acupressure and what modern research can and cannot support.</p><section id="mission" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Our Mission</h2><p class="mb-4">Acupressure Guide was created to provide accurate, practical, and scientifically-informed information about acupressure. We believe that everyone should have access to safe, effective self-care tools that can complement conventional healthcare.</p><p class="mb-4">Every article on this site is written with three principles in mind: accuracy (grounded in both TCM tradition and modern research), practicality (clear, actionable instructions you can use immediately), and safety (clear guidance on contraindications and when to seek professional care).</p></section><section id="who-writes" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Who Writes and Reviews This Site</h2><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6"><p class="mb-3">Acupressure Guide is written and edited by a small editorial team focused on health content. No individual on this team is a licensed physician or TCM practitioner, and we do not claim medical credentials we do not hold.</p><p class="mb-3">Instead, our approach is transparent: every article summarizes what the peer-reviewed literature says on a topic, links directly to the verifiable sources (PubMed, Cochrane, and professional bodies) so you can check our claims yourself, and clearly marks the difference between traditional uses, clinical observations, and research-supported evidence.</p><p class="mb-0">Content on this site is for general informational purposes. We are careful to flag red-flag symptoms and advise seeing a clinician when something may be serious — because self-care guidance is only safe when it knows its limits.</p></div></section><section id="editorial" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Editorial Standards</h2><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>All content is reviewed for accuracy before publication and updated when research changes</li><li>Health claims link to verifiable sources — PubMed records, Cochrane reviews, and professional health bodies</li><li>We cite only sources we have opened and verified at the link provided</li><li>Traditional uses are clearly distinguished from evidence-based applications</li><li>Safety information includes clear contraindications and medical disclaimers</li><li>Where evidence is thin, we say so plainly instead of overstating support</li></ul></section><section id="sources" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Our Sources</h2><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Every guide on this site is built from peer-reviewed research and established references rather than opinion. We read the primary literature, link to it directly so you can verify each claim yourself, and we name the limits of the evidence plainly. The external sources we rely on and recommend include:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">PubMed</a> — the U.S. National Library of Medicine's database of biomedical literature, our primary source for study citations.</li><li><a href="https://www.cochrane.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">Cochrane Library</a> — systematic reviews and evidence summaries, including the P6/PC6 nausea review we cite.</li><li><a href="https://www.nhs.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">NHS (UK National Health Service)</a> — plain-language, clinically-reviewed health information.</li><li><a href="https://www.mayoclinic.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">Mayo Clinic</a> — clinician-authored condition and treatment references.</li></ul><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mt-3">We are an independent editorial project and are not affiliated with any of these organizations. Linking to them is a transparency measure so you can cross-check what we write against trusted authorities.</p></section><section id="disclaimer" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Medical Disclaimer</h2><p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">The information on Acupressure Guide is for general informational purposes only. It does not constitute medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always seek the advice of your physician or other qualified health provider with any questions you may have regarding a medical condition. Never disregard professional medical advice or delay in seeking it because of something you have read on this site.</p></section></article>
